    title:'Payments',
    asof:`Last revised ${shortDate(new Date().toISOString().slice(0,10))}, ${new Date().getUTCFullYear()} · newest number ${dateLabel(newest)}`,
    lead:`Mexico is going digital without going cashless. INEGI's 2024 financial-inclusion survey found that ${fmt(FACTS.enifCashSmall.v,1)}% of adults used cash most often for small purchases. SPEI, Banxico's bank-transfer network, processed ${fmt(S.value/1e6,0)} million transfers in ${dateLabel(S.date)}. By transaction count, debit made up ${fmt(debitShare,1)}% of card purchases at terminals in ${quarterLabel(Card.date)}. The payment system is changing much faster than the habit of using cash.<br><br>My base case is more SPEI and debit use, with cash still common. That is good for payment processors and retailers, but it is not the same as a consumer-credit boom. I would change this view if notes and coins fall year over year and the next survey shows a clear drop in cash use. For operators, the near-term opportunity is making transfers and debit easier to accept and reconcile, not asking customers to learn another payment rail.`,
    next:watchFor(/SPEI|CoDi|tarjeta|currency in circulation/i)||"Banxico's monthly SPEI and currency figures; release dates vary.",
    readings:[
      {label:'Everyday purchases paid in cash',value:fmt(FACTS.enifCashSmall.v,1),unit:'%',change:'INEGI survey of adults',context:'Cash use and digital-payment growth can rise at the same time.',source:'INEGI · ENIF 2024',sourceHref:FACTS.enifUrl,date:'2024'},
      {label:'SPEI transfers',value:fmt(LS.value/1e6,0),unit:'million',change:LSP?`${signed(pct(LS.value,LSP.value),1,'%')} from the prior month`:'',context:'Count of transfers, not the peso value moved.',source:sourceName('banxico-spei-operaciones'),sourceHref:sourceUrl('banxico-spei-operaciones'),date:dateLabel(LS.date),href:'/chart.html?v=banxico-spei-operaciones'},
      {label:'Debit share of terminal purchases',value:fmt(LDebit.value/LCard.value*100,1),unit:'%',change:quarterLabel(LCard.date),context:'Debit-led growth digitizes spending; it is not the same as more consumer credit.',source:sourceName('banxico-tpv-debito-ops'),sourceHref:sourceUrl('banxico-tpv-debito-ops'),date:quarterLabel(LCard.date),href:'/chart.html?v=banxico-tpv-debito-ops,banxico-tpv-credito-ops'},
      {label:'Notes and coins in circulation',value:`MX$${fmt(LC.value/1e6,2)}`,unit:'tn',change:LCY?`${signed(pct(LC.value,LCY.value),1,'%')} from a year earlier`:'',context:'A stock of cash, not a direct measure of how often people pay with it.',source:sourceName('banxico-circulante'),sourceHref:sourceUrl('banxico-circulante'),date:dateLabel(LC.date),href:'/chart.html?v=banxico-circulante'}
    ],
    sections:[
      {h2:`Cash in circulation grew ${tCirc.known?fmt(Math.abs(tCirc.pct),1)+'%':''} in a year`,
       prose:[`The cash itself is growing. Mexicans held <b>MX$${fmt(C.value/1e6,2)} trillion</b> in notes and coins in ${dateLabel(C.date)}${tCirc.known&&Math.abs(tCirc.pct)>=0.5?`, ${fmt(Math.abs(tCirc.pct),1)}% ${tCirc.pct>0?'more':'less'} than a year earlier`:''}. ${cashHolds?'That is not a currency being abandoned. Digital payments are rising and paper is rising with them. The two are not splitting a fixed pot.':'The pile is no longer growing, which is the first sign the habit is loosening.'} Habits at this scale move over years, not quarters.`],
